                            {
                                "target_word": "event",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "44922c02-8381-3da8-a2cb-23a822fab322",
                                        "entry_id": "13e11ce6-b0f3-37ec-9186-efc16db5232d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To occur, take place."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "98942437-c719-4dc9-9806-823a023e56a3",
                                                "sense_id": "44922c02-8381-3da8-a2cb-23a822fab322",
                                                "text": "1590, Robert Greene, Greene’s Never Too Late, in The Life and Complete Works in Prose and Verse of Robert Greene, Volume 8, Huff Library, 1881, p. 33,\n[…] I will first rehearse you an English Historie acted and evented in my Countrey of England […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "event",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "79b1aefd-667d-3978-ae0e-2fb11fce821f",
                                        "entry_id": "15c48697-1609-3466-8045-d21679b65e07",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To be emitted or breathed out; to evaporate."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2190ac9a-298f-4557-8840-302ce1fda77a",
                                                "sense_id": "79b1aefd-667d-3978-ae0e-2fb11fce821f",
                                                "text": "c. 1597, Ben Jonson, The Case is Altered, Act V, Scene 8, in C. H. Herford and Percy Simpson (editors), Ben Jonson, Volume 3, Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1927, p. 178,\nô that thou sawst my heart, or didst behold\nThe place from whence that scalding sigh evented."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1e25425c-b408-458e-9656-27ed27b04129",
                                                "sense_id": "79b1aefd-667d-3978-ae0e-2fb11fce821f",
                                                "text": "This is the reason why this water hath no such force when it is carried, as it hath at the spring it self: because the vertue of it consisteth in a spiritual and occulte qualitie, which eventeth and vanisheth by the carriage."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c185b5e1-0664-3d36-8094-76229174371f",
                                        "entry_id": "15c48697-1609-3466-8045-d21679b65e07",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To expose to the air, ventilate."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "61c63ab7-20e6-4b78-bb0b-669ab6ece550",
                                                "sense_id": "c185b5e1-0664-3d36-8094-76229174371f",
                                                "text": "1559, attributed to William Baldwin, “How the Lorde Clyfford for his straunge and abhominable cruelty came to as straunge and sodayne a death” in The Mirror for Magistrates, Part III, edited by Joseph Haslewood, London: Lackington, Allen & Co., 1815, Volume 2, p. 198,\nFor as I would my gorget have undon\nTo event the heat that had mee nigh undone,\nAn headles arrow strake mee through the throte,\nWhere through my soule forsooke his fylthy cote."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "577c5785-9638-4690-a27f-a16c9c658e85",
                                                "sense_id": "c185b5e1-0664-3d36-8094-76229174371f",
                                                "text": "1598, George Chapman, The Third Sestiad, Hero and Leander (completion of the poem begun by Christopher Marlowe),\n[…] as Phœbus throws\nHis beams abroad, though he in clouds be clos’d,\nStill glancing by them till he find oppos’d\nA loose and rorid vapour that is fit\nT’ event his searching beams, and useth it\nTo form a tender twenty-colour’d eye,\nCast in a circle round about the sky […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "62d0da45-a18f-4735-8600-64a051e3f76b",
                        "source_entry_id": "94cd5409-9760-3284-998d-05eb05a8e9f2",
                        "source_sense_id": "371a8e4f-d98b-314f-8a81-d4f5bddcd5d1",
                        "target_lang_word": "battle",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "battle",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ce279b5a-4df0-37a3-8914-ee5f7f90bb23",
                                        "entry_id": "240739f5-f757-35f5-9954-e17ce97c26e7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A contest, a struggle."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b0951a65-9d07-4079-8e80-c64a72ca8c4e",
                                                "sense_id": "ce279b5a-4df0-37a3-8914-ee5f7f90bb23",
                                                "text": "the battle of life"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f17aca09-3ceb-4fcf-9b62-76c984c29b4a",
                                                "sense_id": "ce279b5a-4df0-37a3-8914-ee5f7f90bb23",
                                                "text": "I returned, and saw vnder the Sunne, That the race is not to the swift, nor the battell to the strong, neither yet bread to the wise, nor yet riches to men of vnderstanding, nor yet fauour to men of skil; but time and chance happeneth to them all."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9994e468-8701-450e-a812-5e339ee246ea",
                                                "sense_id": "ce279b5a-4df0-37a3-8914-ee5f7f90bb23",
                                                "text": "[T]he whole intellectual battle that had at its centre the best poem of the best poet of that day, and had the English Revolution among issues of the strife, was quickening the energies within young Foe's [i.e., Daniel Defoe's] mind when his age was twenty."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7872eb88-bf57-4d84-8153-a0f07c795f88",
                                                "sense_id": "ce279b5a-4df0-37a3-8914-ee5f7f90bb23",
                                                "text": "In truth, Tottenham never really looked like taking all three points and this defeat means they face a battle to reach the knockout stages – with their next home game against PAOK Salonika on 30 November likely to prove decisive."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f7ae7620-c46b-4f11-9578-d19e9f79d742",
                                                "sense_id": "ce279b5a-4df0-37a3-8914-ee5f7f90bb23",
                                                "text": "Australian broadcaster Clive James has admitted that he is losing his long-fought battle with leukaemia."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e81931fe-4f20-3159-b22c-db17ca0a49fd",
                                        "entry_id": "240739f5-f757-35f5-9954-e17ce97c26e7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A contest, a struggle.",
                                            "A one-on-one competition in rapping or breakdance."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f60af153-5b83-3d4f-bd50-7854c87fef97",
                                        "entry_id": "240739f5-f757-35f5-9954-e17ce97c26e7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A general action, fight, or encounter, in which all the divisions of an army are or may be engaged; a combat, an engagement."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5ba9d8b9-6ab1-3bf1-8686-a4332a4ae0fb",
                                        "entry_id": "240739f5-f757-35f5-9954-e17ce97c26e7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A division of an army; a battalion."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "archaic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2bf98f7e-6cc4-499a-a008-e5034689f581",
                                                "sense_id": "5ba9d8b9-6ab1-3bf1-8686-a4332a4ae0fb",
                                                "text": "Thenne kyng Arthur made redy his hooſt in x batails and Nero was redy in the felde afore the caſtel Tarabil with a grete hooſt / & he had x batails with many mo peple than Arthur had […]",
                                                "translation": "Then King Arthur made ready his host in 10 battles and Nero was ready in the field before the castle Tarabil with a great host / and he had 10 battles with many more people than Arthur had […]",
                                                "english": "Then King Arthur made ready his host in 10 battles and Nero was ready in the field before the castle Tarabil with a great host / and he had 10 battles with many more people than Arthur had […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8482d5ad-04d1-45bb-b534-1f406b4b421f",
                                                "sense_id": "5ba9d8b9-6ab1-3bf1-8686-a4332a4ae0fb",
                                                "text": "They ſay, that the King diuided his Armie into three Battailes; whereof the Vant-guard onely well ſtrengthened with wings, came to fight."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "162d49ce-2d8c-4de3-a8d0-0eee526b1637",
                                                "sense_id": "5ba9d8b9-6ab1-3bf1-8686-a4332a4ae0fb",
                                                "text": "Already Cæſar\nHas ravaged more than half the Globe, and ſees\nMankind grown thin by his deſtructive Sword:\nShould he go further, Numbers would be wanting\nTo form new Battels, and ſupport his Crimes."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b0866612-8497-4967-8bc2-07117f4ee352",
                                                "sense_id": "5ba9d8b9-6ab1-3bf1-8686-a4332a4ae0fb",
                                                "text": "No gentleman would appear in the field but on horſeback. To ſerve in any other manner he would have deemed derogatory to his rank. The cavalry, by way of distinction, was called The battle, and on it alone depended the fate of every action. The infantry, collected from the dregs and refuſe of the people, ill armed and worſe diſciplined; was of no account."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d5e90f72-a5e6-4f8e-bd9a-eb6bf321fb54",
                                                "sense_id": "5ba9d8b9-6ab1-3bf1-8686-a4332a4ae0fb",
                                                "text": "Once I link up with Lord Bolton and the Freys, I will have more than twelve thousand men. I mean to divide them into three battles and start up the causeway a half-day apart."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "067a8cd6-2686-3d16-a6a2-5236e07cb3c8",
                                        "entry_id": "240739f5-f757-35f5-9954-e17ce97c26e7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The main body of an army, as distinct from the vanguard and rear; the battalia."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "975d6500-ae34-4cc4-a6c6-e0c226e734a3",
                                                "sense_id": "067a8cd6-2686-3d16-a6a2-5236e07cb3c8",
                                                "text": "Iohn Duke of Norfolke, Thomas Earle of Surrey,\nShall haue the leading of the Foot and Horſe.\nThey thus directed, we will fllow ^([sic])\nIn the maine Battell, whose puiſſance on either ſide\nShall be well-winged with our cheefeſt Horſe:\nThis, and Saint George to boote."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5713a2bf-79f8-45d4-add5-3ac2233f3894",
                                                "sense_id": "067a8cd6-2686-3d16-a6a2-5236e07cb3c8",
                                                "text": "hovering aloofe in the fields he suffered Wyat his Van and main Battell (cutting off some of the Reare) to march undisturbed save with one shot, from Knights-Bridge to Charing Chrosse."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "872e2e37-990e-4408-8546-bb25f0f52b0b",
                                                "sense_id": "067a8cd6-2686-3d16-a6a2-5236e07cb3c8",
                                                "text": "The artillery, under Waldmann, with whom Herter rode in company, was divided between the vanguard and the “battle,” or main body, which comprised the bulk of the army."
                                            }
                                        ]
